Z||000015||terryk||07-07-2000||08:32 PM||||Not a f**king hope in hell. That's doom waiting to happen. Seems that some things should be fixable though.<P>You can try wiring the PRNDL decode to the ECM for 1st gear. No downshift is possible, so it might clear the delay. <P>The ABM (Abusive Manuever Mode) might/could kick in though. Unless something has changed on this, the ECM will watch for the brake pedal being pressed (if you connected that wire for cruise) and the RPM being up and the MPH being 0. It knows you're thinking of burning them off and will back off the spark and possibly bypass the blower some to save the trans. I'll have to think about it a bit. On the older SC code, ABM goes off at ab0out 10MPH. Actually, ABM might be a good thing for the Getrag. <P>Too bad the manual wasn't available with the 3800. Oh well.<P>Of course it could be a totally different problem. I know that the people that are doing these swaps commercially have come up with some clever solutions.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-07-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000016||cobra427||07-07-2000||09:12 PM||||RobertVol,<BR>Here is another check that you may want to check as well.The throttle position sensor needs to be properly adjusted as well.It is not like the 2.8.(just remove and replace.no adjustment)The 3.8 TPS is slotted a little and when you replace it, it has to be adjusted.I have an 88 Olds 3.8 and I'm not sure if the setting is exactly the same as your OBD ll system but the adjustment on mine is as such:<BR>1.With the TPS attacking screws loose,install<BR>3 jumper wires between TPS and harness conector.<BR>2.With the ignition switch ON, use a digital<BR>voltmeter connected to terminals B and C and  adjust TPS to obtain 0.40 + - 0.05 volts.<BR>3.Tighten the attaching screws, then recheck reading to insure the adjustment has not moved. (You will want to use a thread locking compound =Loctite 262)<BR>4.With the ignition switch OFF, remove the jumper wires and connect the harness to the TPS<BR>I just had this happen to me. The little roll pin that is attached to the throttle shaft where the blade of the TPS rest against<BR>somehow was missing (broke off) which put everything else out of sync. Once a new pin was installed, I was back in business<P>Just another thought thats all.<P>------------------<BR>Jody,86.5 GT<BR>The UN is not your "Friend"||||reg||1
Z||000017||lowCG||07-07-2000||10:42 PM||||Terry,that's what I was thinking about the first gear assignment,didn't know if other things would be limited(timing,mixture)in this position though(?) I recall that the late model BMW I rode in had some obvious impairment of these(fuel/ignition)systems while in first gear,I assumed it was to save the gearbox,since there wasn't a traction control system on that car.<BR>I've done alright by just fooling the ecm in the past,actually great,maintaining driveability,but this might be quite a challenging system from what it sounds.<BR>I set mine up to be in drive,since that was where it was with all the position switch wires put together,seemed like the hot-rodder way that the design engineers at GM had intended in their master plan of things.<BR>I wonder how assigning reverse would work?||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||07-07-2000||11:02 PM||||LowCG,<P>With the auto, the anti abuse mode will kick in if the brake is pressed, RPM is up and the car is not moving. With that manual on there, it shouldn't go in to ABM. Yes, that is to save the trans.<P>I'm working on the DOHC code. What year is the ECM? 96 or up? <P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-07-2000).]||||reg||1
